<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RELEASE network policy. Debug builds use src/debug/res/xml/network_security_config.xml
  instead (Android's resource merger lets the debug source set override this file).

  H-02: this file is inert unless <application android:networkSecurityConfig> points at
  it. A guard in app/build.gradle fails release builds if that attribute is removed.

  H-03: certificate pinning below.

  Pin values were computed from the live certificate chains, not copied from a template:
      openssl s_client -servername <host> -connect <host>:443 -showcerts </dev/null \
        | openssl x509 -pubkey -noout \
        | openssl pkey -pubin -outform der \
        | openssl dgst -sha256 -binary | openssl enc -base64

  WHAT IS PINNED, AND WHY THAT LEVEL:

  Both api.exchangapay.com and tstapi.exchangapay.com chain through the same DigiCert
  intermediate, so a single pin covers both:

      leaf   api.exchangapay.com      iIJcl/fDdBtX2PZaQXIEsip+r/eSKnx4btuU3j6Nn7M=  exp 2026-12-13
      leaf   tstapi.exchangapay.com   pOlyhLUFmEV1ozZhaFDJJi7qp4gTXq58jsZVTdtWzD0=
      inter  GeoTrust TLS RSA CA G1   SDG5orEv8iX6MNenIAxa8nQFNpROB/6+llsZdXHZNqs=  exp 2027-11-02
      root   DigiCert Global Root G2  i7WTqTvh0OioIruIfFR4kMPnBqrS2rdiVPl/s2uC/CY=  exp 2038-01-15

  The intermediate is pinned, never the leaf: leaf pins break on every certificate
  renewal, and the prod leaf expires 2026-12-13. The DigiCert root is the backup pin,
  so a reissue from a different DigiCert intermediate still validates instead of
  bricking installed apps. Two pins are required — one pin plus one renewal is an
  outage with no recovery path short of a store release.

  NOT PINNED, DELIBERATELY:

  H-02: this list used to live here, in prose, and it was incomplete — it named the
  third parties somebody had thought about and omitted six hosts nobody knew existed,
  because they were hardcoded in src/utils/api.tsx. An omission and a decision looked
  identical from inside this file.

  The list now lives in security/pinning-policy.json, one entry per host with a reason,
  a remediation and a review date, and it is ENFORCED: a release build fails if any host
  in environments/*.js is neither pinned below nor exempt there, and fails again if an
  exemption has no reason or its review date has passed. See the H-02 coverage guard in
  app/build.gradle and __tests__/pinningCoverage.test.ts. Read that file rather than
  trusting this comment to stay current.

  In short, the obsolete development, Azure and Suissebase hosts were removed from the
  app configuration. Auth0, Sentry, Sumsub and CoinGecko remain third-party services
  with independently rotating certificates; their documented exemptions are reviewed
  in security/pinning-policy.json.

  ANY HOST ADDED TO environments/*.js MUST BE PINNED HERE, ON iOS, OR EXEMPTED.
  Compute the hashes with ./scripts/compute-spki-pins.sh; add to both platforms or the
  Android/iOS parity check fails the build.

  BEFORE THE EXPIRATION DATE BELOW: re-run ./scripts/compute-spki-pins.sh, confirm the
  intermediate pin still matches, and extend the date. After it passes, Android silently
  stops enforcing pinning and falls back to normal validation — the app keeps working,
  but the H-03 protection is gone until the date is renewed.

  LAST VERIFIED AGAINST THE LIVE CHAINS: 2026-08-05. Both hosts still chain through
  GeoTrust TLS RSA CA G1, whose SPKI matches the primary pin; that intermediate is
  issued by DigiCert Global Root G2, whose SPKI matches the backup pin. The recovery
  path is confirmed, not assumed. Findings recorded in security/pinning-policy.json.
-->
<network-security-config>
  <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
    <trust-anchors>
      <certificates src="system" />
    </trust-anchors>
  </base-config>

  <domain-config>
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">api.exchangapay.com</domain>
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">tstapi.exchangapay.com</domain>
    <!-- M-03 — this date MUST equal pinSet.androidExpiration in
         security/pinning-policy.json, which is the source of truth. An XML
         attribute cannot read JSON, so the value is necessarily written twice;
         the H-03 guard in android/app/build.gradle fails the release build if
         the two ever disagree. Change the policy file first, then copy it here.

         Note this date is EARLIER than the intermediate's real expiry, on
         purpose. Android fails OPEN here — past this date it silently stops
         enforcing pinning, with no build error and no runtime symptom — so the
         date is a review deadline set ahead of the cliff, not the cliff. -->
    <pin-set expiration="2027-06-01">
      <!-- primary: GeoTrust TLS RSA CA G1 (issuing intermediate) -->
      <pin digest="SHA-256">SDG5orEv8iX6MNenIAxa8nQFNpROB/6+llsZdXHZNqs=</pin>
      <!-- backup: DigiCert Global Root G2 -->
      <pin digest="SHA-256">i7WTqTvh0OioIruIfFR4kMPnBqrS2rdiVPl/s2uC/CY=</pin>
    </pin-set>
  </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
